msgid "API"
msgstr ""

#: block/charger.md
msgid "Programs can check on the charger through the `charger` device. See the [charger device]({1}) reference for its methods."
msgstr ""

#: block/computer.md
msgid "Computer"
msgstr ""

#: block/computer.md
msgid "![1 + 1 = 10]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: block/computer.md
msgid "Computers provide a basis for building hardware capable of running an operating system or low-level software."
msgstr ""

#: block/computer.md
msgid "Read the [getting started guide]({1}) for a short explanation on how to get a working computer."
msgstr ""

#: block/computer.md
#: block/terminal.md
msgid "Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: block/computer.md
msgid "Configure the components in a computer in its inventory screen. To access this screen, either open the computer with a [wrench]({1}), or switch to the inventory screen using the toggle button to the left of the terminal screen."
msgstr ""

#: block/computer.md
msgid "Connectivity"
msgstr ""

#: block/computer.md
msgid "To connect external devices to a computer, use [bus interfaces]({1}) and [bus cables]({2}). Note that computers must be explicitly connected to a bus with a bus interface."
msgstr ""

#: block/computer.md
msgid "Usually, computers will require energy to run. Most modern energy generators producing RF energy should be compatible. Computers have a small built-in energy storage. However, this will usually not be sufficient to keep the machine running for any prolonged amount of time."
msgstr ""

#: block/disk_drive.md
msgid "Disk Drive"
msgstr ""

#: block/disk_drive.md
msgid "![Taking it for a spin]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: block/disk_drive.md
msgid "The disk drive provides an option for quick media exchange. [Floppy disks]({1}) can be added and removed at runtime, unlike [hard drives]({2})."
msgstr ""

#: block/disk_drive.md
#: block/keyboard.md
#: block/projector.md
#: item/hard_drive.md
#: item/memory.md
#: item/network_interface_card.md
#: item/network_tunnel_card.md
#: item/serial_interface_card.md
#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "Computers *have to be shut down* before installing or removing this component. Installing it while the computer is running will have no effect, removing it may lead to system errors."
msgstr ""

#: block/disk_drive.md
msgid "See the [OpenOS II guide]({1}) for more on drives. Disk drives appear as `/dev/vdX` devices, following any installed hard drives. Floppies are mounted automatically, at `/mnt/<label>` or at `/mnt/<n>` without a label. Run `format /dev/vdX` to format a new floppy. Close open files before removing a floppy, to avoid data loss."
msgstr ""

#: block/disk_drive.md
msgid "See the [Linux guide]({1}) for more on drives. Disk drives appear as `/dev/vdX` devices, following any installed hard drives. They are not automatically formatted or mounted, they will only appear as raw block devices:"
msgstr ""

#: block/disk_drive.md
msgid "`mke2fs /dev/vdX` formats a floppy disk. Run this when first installing the disk. *Will erase data on floppy!*"
msgstr ""

#: block/disk_drive.md
msgid "`mount /dev/vdX <mount directory>` mounts a floppy disk after formatting it. Make sure the directory you want to mount it to exists and is empty."
msgstr ""

#: block/disk_drive.md
msgid "`umount <mount directory>` unmounts a floppy disk. Make sure to run this before removing the floppy from the disk drive, to avoid data loss."
msgstr ""

#: block/disk_drive.md
#: block/terminal.md
#: item/serial_interface_card.md
#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "CP/M"
msgstr ""

#: block/disk_drive.md
msgid "See the [CP/M guide]({1}) for more on drives. Drives are lettered in the order they are found, starting at `A:`. The disk the machine booted from always comes first."
msgstr ""

#: block/flash_drive.md
msgid "Flash Drive"
msgstr ""

#: block/flash_drive.md
msgid "![Savior of the Universe]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: block/flash_drive.md
msgid "The flash drive exposes [flash memory]({1}) to a [computer]({2}) as a plain block device. Where the [disk drive]({3}) provides access to floppies, this one provides access to flash memory. In particular, this enables use of custom firmware."
msgstr ""

#: block/flash_drive.md
msgid "Flash memory can be inserted and removed at runtime. The drive itself cannot: computers *have to be shut down* before installing or removing it."
msgstr ""

#: block/flash_drive.md
msgid "On OpenOS II and Linux, the drive appears as a `/dev/vdX` device, following any installed hard drives, just like a floppy disk drive. It is a raw block device. CP/M does not support it."
msgstr ""

#: block/flash_drive.md
msgid "To write your firmware to the flash memory, *erasing what was there before*:"
msgstr ""

#: block/index.md
msgid "Blocks"
msgstr ""

#: block/index.md
msgid "This index lists all documented blocks. If you're looking for an item, see the [item index]({1}) instead."
msgstr ""

#: block/index.md
msgid "To quickly access the manual entry for a block in the world, simply use this [manual]({1}) on the block."
msgstr ""

#: block/index.md
msgid "Note: most device blocks accept a bus connection on one face only, the one with a port on it; a [bus interface]({1}) can only connect to this face. There are exceptions, such as the [redstone interface]({2}) and the [transposer]({3}), which connect from multiple sides."
