#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "Internet Gateway"
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "![Wired]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "The internet gateway connects a network of [network connectors]({1}) to the world outside the game, so every [computer]({2}) on that network can use it. Attach it to a [network connector]({3}) the same way a computer is attached."
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "**Servers decide whether this works at all.** Operators choose which addresses are allowed, and can switch it off entirely."
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "The gateway consumes energy for every packet it transmits, in both directions, and drops packets when insufficiently powered."
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "Setting Up a Computer"
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "Computers access the gateway through an ordinary [network interface card]({1}). Note that only [Linux]({2}) supports networking."
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "The gateway has a configurable address, `192.168.0.254` by default. Open the settings screen by using it."
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "The gateway does not provide a DHCP service, so computers have to be assigned addresses explicitly, or by running a DHCP server on one of the computers. To use the gateway with a manually set address:"
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "It also does not provide name servers, so set one in `/etc/resolv.conf`. Name lookups then travel as ordinary traffic, and are subject to the same address restrictions as everything else."
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "The commands above last until the next reboot. `setup-network.lua` does all of this and writes it to `/etc/network/interfaces`, so it survives a reboot. It will also offer to run a DHCP server on the computer. Every other computer on the network then needs no setup at all: it asks the DHCP server for an address, the route out and a name server."
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "TCP, UDP and ping are supported. Connections can only be opened outwards: nothing on the internet can connect to a computer."
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "`https://` addresses work, but **without certificate verification**, because Linux does not ship a list of trusted authorities. The traffic is protected from passive observers, but there is no guarantee the remote is not a man-in-the-middle. Treat it accordingly, and do not send passwords you care about through it."
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "Connections do not survive the chunk unloading or the server restarting. Treat this as a temporary connection failure and handle it accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "VXLAN"
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "A second mode can be enabled in the mods' server configuration, Virtual Extensible LAN (VXLAN). In this mode the gateway connects the computers to a network that the server operator set up outside the game. Computers then appear on that network directly, without network address translation (NAT) or the restrictions above. Whether that network provides addresses, routes and name servers depends on its setup."
msgstr ""

#: block/internet_gateway.md
msgid "When available, switch the mode in the gateway's settings screen, then enter the remote peer, port and VXLAN network identifier (VNI). Ask your server operator for these values."
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "Keyboard"
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "![To click or not to click]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "The keyboard allows key presses to be picked up by [computers]({1}). Keyboards may be placed anywhere, as long as they're connected to the computer via a bus. CP/M does not support them."
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "To start typing on a keyboard, simply interact with it. Experienced typists will find they do not need to look at the keys while typing (you can still look around while using the keyboard). Interact again to stop using the keyboard."
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "Unlike the terminal screen, keyboards send key positions (scancodes), which the computer interprets using its own layout, defaulting to US. If that gives you the wrong characters, load a matching layout as described below."
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "See the [OpenOS II guide]({1}) for general usage."
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "OpenOS II does not show a console on a projector, the keyboard has to be used by programs manually. Each key press arrives as a `key_down` signal, each release as a `key_up` signal. Run `keytest` to see the values. In Lua:"
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "`char` is the character as a number, `-1` for keys without one. `code` matches the Linux key code."
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "To load another layout, `us`, `de`, `fr` or `uk`:"
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "The layout is reset on the next reboot. Add it to `/.shrc` to load it automatically."
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "See the [Linux guide]({1}) for general usage."
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "The keyboard provides events via the `evdev` system. Its primary use-case is in combination with a [projector]({1}): key presses are sent to the terminal session displayed there, without additional configuration."
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "To load another layout:"
msgstr ""

#: block/keyboard.md
msgid "Check that directory for a list of available layouts."
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "Network Connector"
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "![The data must flow]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "The network connector is a fundamental part for building a network connecting different [computers]({1}). Use [network cables]({2}) to connect connectors with each other."
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "Each connector will represent one \"hop\" for relayed packets. Packets may only travel a limited number of hops before they are dropped."
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "Note that each connector only supports up to two attached network cables. Chain multiple connectors to increase the range that can be spanned."
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "Create a simple multi-computer network by chaining the connectors attached to the computers. To create a mesh network, use a [network hub]({1}). The hub connects all attached connectors with each other."
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "Third-Party Computers"
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "Connectors may be used to enable serial communication with computers manufactured by other vendors. When attached to a third-party computer, the connector will provide an endpoint that allows sending and receiving serial data."
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "ComputerCraft"
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "To connect to computers of the ComputerCraft brand, connectors may be mounted on computers and will be recognized as a peripheral of type `oc2_serial_interface`. Sends data using `write(data)`; it which returns how many bytes fit into the send buffer. Read data using `read([count])`; it returns the received bytes along with how many of them were corrupted, or nothing when there was no data."
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "`available()` allows querying how much data is ready, `clear()` drops the receive buffer."
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "When new data arrives, the event `oc2_serial_data` is queued with the peripheral's name. Use this to avoid polling."
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "The baud rate and address are read and set with `getBaudRate()`, `setBaudRate(rate)` for rates from 300 to 115200, `getAddress()` and `setAddress(address)` for addresses from 0 to 254, and the error counters of the [serial device]({1}) are available as well. After a reload the peripheral is reset to 9600 baud with a fresh address. Set them up on program start."
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "TIS-3D"
msgstr ""

#: block/network_connector.md
msgid "For the TIS-3D computer, a connector mounted on a serial port module will enable data exchange. Each value written is sent as one byte, each byte received is read as one value, with the high byte non-zero when the byte was corrupted on the line. For further details, see the protocol's page in the TIS-3D manual."
msgstr ""

#: block/network_hub.md
msgid "Network Hub"
msgstr ""

#: block/network_hub.md
msgid "![One to five]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: block/network_hub.md
msgid "The network hub connects multiple [network connectors]({1}) with each other. It forwards any packet arriving at any attached connector to all other attached connectors. This forwarding operation will consume one hop, reducing the packet's time to live."
msgstr ""

#: block/projector.md
msgid "Projector"
msgstr ""

#: block/projector.md
msgid "![Shadow on the wall]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: block/projector.md
msgid "The projector provides a framebuffer device to [computers]({1}). They have a resolution of 640 by 480 pixels, with the color format r5g6b5: 5 bits for the red color component, 6 bits for the green color component and 5 bits for the blue color component."
msgstr ""

#: block/projector.md
msgid "Projectors need to be powered directly to function. The device bus cannot provide enough energy on its own. When insufficiently powered, this is indicated by a red glow in the projector's lens."
msgstr ""

#: block/projector.md
msgid "On OpenOS II and Linux, projectors appear as `/dev/fbX` devices. To send data to the framebuffer, write to these devices. For example, to clear a framebuffer, write 614400 zeros to it, two bytes for each pixel: `head -c 614400 /dev/zero > /dev/fb0`. CP/M does not support projectors."
msgstr ""

#: block/projector.md
msgid "On Linux, the first connected framebuffer shows a virtual terminal, and a [keyboard]({1}) may be used to send input to it."
msgstr ""

#: block/redstone_interface.md
msgid "Redstone Interface"
msgstr ""

#: block/redstone_interface.md
msgid "![Everything Red]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: block/redstone_interface.md
msgid "The redstone interface provides an omnidirectional bus to receive and emit redstone signals."
msgstr ""

#: block/redstone_interface.md
msgid "Use this to interact with primitive devices, such as doors and lamps, or other machinery offering a redstone based protocol."
msgstr ""

#: block/redstone_interface.md
msgid "Programs control the redstone interface through the `redstone` device. See the [redstone device]({1}) reference for its methods and how sides are named."
msgstr ""

#: block/terminal.md
msgid "Terminal"
msgstr ""

#: block/terminal.md
msgid "![Remote viewing]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: block/terminal.md
msgid "A terminal is a standalone screen and keyboard without the rest of the computer, connected to a serial bus using a [network connector]({1}). It reads and writes serial data verbatim. Use a [serial card]({2}) in a computer to enable interaction with the computer."
msgstr ""

#: block/terminal.md
msgid "The console can be moved to a terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: block/terminal.md
msgid "The shell then runs on the terminal, and the computer's own screen stays unchanged. It can be moved back using `console /dev/ttyS0`. To do this automatically at boot time, in `/init.d/50-console.lua`:"
msgstr ""

#: block/terminal.md
msgid "The default on both ends is 9600 baud. For faster output, set the same rate in the terminal's settings and with `stty -F /dev/ttyS1 115200`."
msgstr ""

#: block/terminal.md
msgid "Use `getty` to spawn a session on the terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: block/terminal.md
msgid "Switch to an external terminal like so:"
msgstr ""

#: block/terminal.md
msgid "Configure the terminal address and baud rate in its settings screen. To access this screen, either open the terminal with a [wrench]({1}), or switch to the settings screen using the toggle button on the left of the terminal screen."
msgstr ""

#: block/transposer.md
#: device/transposer.md
msgid "Transposer"
msgstr ""

#: block/transposer.md
msgid "![Less posing, more rights]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: block/transposer.md
msgid "The transposer moves items and fluids between containers next to it. This includes chests, cauldrons and machines supporting automation. Moving to or from an unblocked side drops items into the world and picks them up again, or fills with and drains fluids."
msgstr ""

#: block/transposer.md
msgid "It can be controlled using both the [high-level API]({1}) and the [mid-level API]({2}). For example:"
msgstr ""

#: block/transposer.md
msgid "Programs control the transposer through the `transposer` device. See the [transposer device]({1}) reference for its methods."
msgstr ""

