#: item/block_operations_module.md
msgid "Block Operations Module"
msgstr ""

#: item/block_operations_module.md
msgid "![Break it, quick replace it]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/block_operations_module.md
msgid "The block operations module provides to [robots]({1}) the ability to break and place blocks in the world."
msgstr ""

#: item/block_operations_module.md
msgid "Programs control the module through the `block_operations` device. See the [block operations device]({1}) reference for its methods."
msgstr ""

#: item/cpu_riscv.md
msgid "RISC-V Processor"
msgstr ""

#: item/cpu_riscv.md
msgid "![The part that does the thinking.]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/cpu_riscv.md
#: item/cpu_z80.md
msgid "The processor is what a [computer]({1}) or [robot]({2}) actually computes with. Without one in its processor slot, a machine will not start."
msgstr ""

#: item/cpu_riscv.md
msgid "This is the 64-bit RISC-V processor. It runs [OpenOS II]({1}) and [Linux]({2}). Crafting it together with blank flash memory or a hard drive writes the firmware or an operating system to them, see the guides for details."
msgstr ""

#: item/cpu_riscv.md
msgid "Also see the [Z80 processor]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: item/cpu_z80.md
msgid "Z80 Processor"
msgstr ""

#: item/cpu_z80.md
msgid "![Part think.]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/cpu_z80.md
msgid "This is an 8-bit Z80 processor. It runs [CP/M]({1}). It comes with 64K of built-in memory, so it needs very little hardware around it. Devices available through the [MLAPI]({2}), such as the [redstone interface]({3}), work as expected. [Memory]({4}) modules, hard drives, network cards and the other native devices go unused, as the Z80 has no use or drivers for them. They can stay on the bus regardless."
msgstr ""

#: item/cpu_z80.md
msgid "Crafting it with blank flash memory writes the CP/M loader. Crafting it with a blank floppy writes the CP/M system."
msgstr ""

#: item/cpu_z80.md
msgid "Also see the [RISC-V processor]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: item/crafting_module.md
msgid "Crafting Module"
msgstr ""

#: item/crafting_module.md
msgid "![Some assembly required]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/crafting_module.md
msgid "The crafting module provides to [robots]({1}) the ability to craft items. The top part of the robot's inventory serves as the crafting grid. Crafted items are inserted into the bottom row, starting at the selected slot."
msgstr ""

#: item/crafting_module.md
msgid "Programs control the module through the `crafting` device. See the [crafting device]({1}) reference for its methods."
msgstr ""

#: item/file_import_export_card.md
msgid "File Import/Export Card"
msgstr ""

#: item/file_import_export_card.md
msgid "![Tear down this wall!]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/file_import_export_card.md
msgid "The file import/export card allows breaking the fourth wall. It allows:"
msgstr ""

#: item/file_import_export_card.md
msgid "uploading files from your real computer into a virtual [computer]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: item/file_import_export_card.md
msgid "downloading files from a virtual computer onto your real computer."
msgstr ""

#: item/file_import_export_card.md
msgid "For added convenience, OpenOS II and Linux provide two utility scripts for these operations, `import.lua` and `export.lua`. On OpenOS II, they can be run as `import` and `export`. CP/M does not support this card."
msgstr ""

#: item/file_import_export_card.md
msgid "`import.lua` will, when run, prompt for a file to upload to the virtual computer. The file will be stored in the current working directory. If a file with the name of the imported file already exists, the option to provide an alternative name will be offered."
msgstr ""

#: item/file_import_export_card.md
msgid "`export.lua` takes as its parameter the path to a file in a virtual computer. It then downloads this file to your real computer and offers a save dialog, offering the choice of where to save the downloaded file, or to cancel the operation."
msgstr ""

#: item/file_import_export_card.md
msgid "Both scripts will prompt all users currently interacting with the computer's terminal. For upload operations (`import.lua`), the first uploaded file will be used. The prompts on other clients will be canceled. For download operations all clients will be offered to save the exported file."
msgstr ""

#: item/file_import_export_card.md
msgid "Programs drive the card through the `file_import_export` device, which is what `import.lua` and `export.lua` use. See the [file import/export device]({1}) reference for its methods."
msgstr ""

#: item/flash_memory.md
msgid "Flash Memory"
msgstr ""

#: item/flash_memory.md
msgid "![Not that Flash]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/flash_memory.md
msgid "Flash memory contains the initial code loaded into a [computer's]({1}) memory upon boot. It holds the firmware, which is just large enough to find the system it starts and load it from a drive."
msgstr ""

#: item/flash_memory.md
msgid "Blank flash memory holds no firmware. To write firmware to it, craft it together with the processor that firmware is built for: a [RISC-V processor]({1}) writes the Sedna firmware, which boots OpenOS II and Linux, a [Z80 processor]({2}) writes the CP/M firmware."
msgstr ""

#: item/flash_memory.md
msgid "Neither firmware includes a system of its own, so a machine needs the matching medium as well: a [hard drive]({1}) for OpenOS II or Linux, a [floppy]({2}) for CP/M."
msgstr ""

#: item/flash_memory.md
msgid "You are not limited to the supplied firmwares. A [flash drive]({1}) provides flash memory to a computer as a plain block device, so a machine can write whatever you like onto it."
msgstr ""

#: item/flash_memory.md
msgid "Crafting programmed flash memory with a [wrench]({1}) erases it back to blank flash memory, discarding the firmware and anything stored on it."
msgstr ""

#: item/floppy.md
msgid "Floppy"
msgstr ""

#: item/floppy.md
msgid "![It's not the floppy that bends]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/floppy.md
msgid "Floppy disks are removable media. This means they can be inserted and removed from [disk drives]({1}) without the [computer]({2}) using the disk drive having to reboot to use them."
msgstr ""

#: item/floppy.md
msgid "Crafting a blank floppy together with a [Z80 processor]({1}) writes the CP/M system to it. That is the disk a Z80 machine boots from. Crafting a written floppy with a [wrench]({2}) erases it back to a blank one."
msgstr ""

#: item/floppy.md
msgid "[Robots]({1}) have a floppy slot, as they cannot use disk drives."
msgstr ""

#: item/floppy.md
msgid "Please see the [disk drive]({1}) manual entry for some useful commands when working with floppies."
msgstr ""

#: item/floppy.md
msgid "Storage media are not perfect, and a floppy left unused for a long time may suffer from bit rot and lose its contents. Format corrupted floppies using the [wrench]({1}). Interestingly, this only seems to happen when there are a lot of storage devices in the world."
msgstr ""

#: item/hard_drive.md
msgid "Hard Drive"
msgstr ""

#: item/hard_drive.md
msgid "![Going in circles]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/hard_drive.md
msgid "Hard drives are the main storage device used for data storage in [computers]({1}) and [robots]({2})."
msgstr ""

#: item/hard_drive.md
msgid "Usually computers use a firmware that requires some hard drive to complete the boot process. See the [flash memory]({1}) entry for more details. Crafting a hard drive together with a [RISC-V processor]({2}) writes an operating system to it:"
msgstr ""

#: item/hard_drive.md
msgid "2M hard drive: [OpenOS II]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: item/hard_drive.md
msgid "8M hard drive: [Linux]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: item/hard_drive.md
msgid "Crafting either of these drives with a [wrench]({1}) wipes it back to an empty drive."
msgstr ""

#: item/hard_drive.md
msgid "OpenOS II and Linux show additional hard drives as `/dev/vdb`, `/dev/vdc` and so on. See the guides on how to format and mount them. CP/M does not support hard drives."
msgstr ""

#: item/hard_drive.md
msgid "Storage media are not perfect, and a drive left unused for a long time may suffer from bit rot and lose its contents. Format corrupted drives using the [wrench]({1}). Interestingly, this only seems to happen when there are a lot of storage devices in the world."
msgstr ""

