#: item/inventory_operations_module.md
msgid "Inventory Operations Module"
msgstr ""

#: item/inventory_operations_module.md
msgid "![What's yours is mine]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/inventory_operations_module.md
msgid "The inventory operations module provides to [robots]({1}) the ability to insert and extract items from inventories in the world. This supports block and entity inventories, as well as container items in the robot's inventory, such as shulker boxes. Without an inventory present, given sufficient space, it allows dropping or taking items into and from the world."
msgstr ""

#: item/inventory_operations_module.md
msgid "Programs control the module through the `inventory_operations` device. See the [inventory operations device]({1}) reference for its methods."
msgstr ""

#: item/manual.md
msgid "![Nobody reads me anyway...]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/manual.md
msgid "You handy quick-reference for all topics concerning [computers]({1}) and devices related to them. It's what you're currently reading!"
msgstr ""

#: item/manual.md
msgid "For convenience, using the manual on any known block in the world will open the corresponding manual page."
msgstr ""

#: item/memory.md
msgid "Memory"
msgstr ""

#: item/memory.md
msgid "![Random Access Memories]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/memory.md
msgid "The random access memory (RAM) device provides working memory to [computers]({1}) and [robots]({2})."
msgstr ""

#: item/memory.md
msgid "As opposed to [hard drives]({1}), this memory is volatile and will be cleared when a computer powers down. It is theoretically possible for computers to function without RAM, by software only using the stack for data storage. However, typically software requires RAM to function. In particular, the [firmware]({2}) contained in newly obtained computers requires a decent amount to function."
msgstr ""

#: item/memory.md
msgid "How much memory is needed depends on the operating system. OpenOS II runs with a single 2M module, Linux needs at least 8M. The [Z80 processor]({1}) has memory built in, so CP/M needs no module. Also see the [getting started]({2}) manual entry."
msgstr ""

#: item/network_cable.md
msgid "Network Cable"
msgstr ""

#: item/network_cable.md
msgid "![What the CAT!?]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/network_cable.md
msgid "Network cables connect pairs of [network connectors]({1}). This makes it possible to conveniently bridge large distances. To place a network cable, use it on the two network connectors to connect, one after the other."
msgstr ""

#: item/network_cable.md
msgid "Cables have a maximum length. It may be necessary to use network connectors as repeaters to increase the effective range of network cables."
msgstr ""

#: item/network_interface_card.md
msgid "Network Interface Card"
msgstr ""

#: item/network_interface_card.md
msgid "![Talk to strangers]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/network_interface_card.md
msgid "The network interface card (NIC) allows [computers]({1}) to send messages to and receive messages from [network connectors]({2}) attached to the computer."
msgstr ""

#: item/network_interface_card.md
#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "These cards can be configured to only connect to selected sides (use while holding). This allows using multiple cards to build a custom router, for example."
msgstr ""

#: item/network_interface_card.md
#: item/network_tunnel_card.md
#: item/network_tunnel_module.md
msgid "Only [Linux]({1}) supports this device. It provides a regular ethernet device. Network setup can either be performed manually, or using the convenience script `setup-network.lua`. This script provides the option to either use a fixed-address setup, or a DHCP setup. For a DHCP setup, exactly one computer in the network must act as a DHCP server. See [networking]({2}) for details."
msgstr ""

#: item/network_interface_card.md
#: item/network_tunnel_card.md
#: item/network_tunnel_module.md
msgid "After initial setup, use the command `ifconfig` to see the currently used IP address."
msgstr ""

#: item/network_tunnel_card.md
msgid "Network Tunnel Card"
msgstr ""

#: item/network_tunnel_card.md
#: item/network_tunnel_module.md
msgid "![Tunnel vision?]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/network_tunnel_card.md
msgid "The network tunnel card allows [computers]({1}) to send messages to and receive messages from another tunnel device (tunnel cards and [tunnel modules]({2})) linked to the card."
msgstr ""

#: item/network_tunnel_card.md
#: item/network_tunnel_module.md
msgid "To link two tunnel devices, open their configuration interface (use while holding), and insert the other tunnel device to link to. This allows linking any two network tunnel devices."
msgstr ""

#: item/network_tunnel_module.md
msgid "Network Tunnel Module"
msgstr ""

#: item/network_tunnel_module.md
msgid "The network tunnel module allows [robots]({1}) to send messages to and receive messages from another tunnel device (tunnel modules and [tunnel cards]({2})) linked to the module."
msgstr ""

#: item/network_tunnel_module.md
msgid "Robots *have to be shut down* before installing or removing this component. Installing it while the robot is running will have no effect, removing it may lead to system errors."
msgstr ""

#: item/redstone_interface_card.md
msgid "Redstone Interface Card"
msgstr ""

#: item/redstone_interface_card.md
msgid "![Got signal?]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/redstone_interface_card.md
msgid "The redstone interface card is a more compact version of the [redstone interface block]({1}). It enables users to build more compact designs."
msgstr ""

#: item/redstone_interface_card.md
msgid "The general functionality is identical to the block version. The front of the [computer]({1}) represents the primary face as described in the redstone interface block's manual entry."
msgstr ""

#: item/redstone_interface_card.md
msgid "Programs control the card through the `redstone` device, the same device the redstone interface block provides. See the [redstone device]({1}) reference for its methods and how sides are named."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "![I, for one, welcome our new robot overlords]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "Robots are essentially mobile [computers]({1}). Due to their non-stationary nature, there is some behavior that differs from regular computers. They cannot connect to [bus interfaces]({2}). Instead of card devices, they support module devices. These are specialized devices taking into account the robots' mobility. In place of a second [hard drive]({3}) bay they have a [floppy]({4}) slot, since they cannot use [disk drives]({5})."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "Robots have a fixed-size inventory, a fluid tank, and sport a state-of-the-art energy storage. Only the regular inventory and the tank of robots can be automatically filled and emptied, for example by a hopper or a pump. The component inventory of the robot must be manually configured."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "In their default configuration, robots cannot interact with their own inventory. Use an [inventory operations module]({1}) to enable robots to move items in their own inventory, as well as to insert and extract items to and from other inventories. Use a [tank operations module]({2}) to do the same with fluids and the robot's tank."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "To recharge a robot, it is recommended to make use the [charger]({1}). It is possible for robots to recharge themselves by simply moving on top of a charger. Alternatively, they may be placed into an inventory on top of the charger."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "OpenOS II and Linux provide a utility Lua library, `robot`, that eases controlling robots. The underlying API offers asynchronous methods for movement. The library implements synchronous alternatives, making sequential programming more convenient."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "For an overview of how robots move, how they draw energy and how to script them, see the [robotics]({1}) entry."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "Programs control the robot through the `robot` device. See the [robot device]({1}) reference for its methods."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "Library"
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "Library name: `robot`"
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "This is a Lua library. It can be used on OpenOS II and Linux. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`facing():string` returns the compass direction the robot is facing."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`position():table` returns the robot's position relative to its origin."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "Returns a table with `x`, `y` and `z`, in blocks. See the \"Position\" section of the [robot device]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`calibrate()` makes the robot's current position its origin."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`energy():number` returns the current amount of energy stored in the robot's internal energy storage."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`capacity():number` returns the maximum amount of energy that can be stored in the robot's internal energy storage."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`slot():number` returns the currently selected robot inventory slot. This is used by many modules as an implicit input."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`slot(slot:number):number` sets the currently selected robot inventory slot. This is used by many modules as an implicit input."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`slot` is the index of the inventory slot to select."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`stack([slot:number]):table` gets a description of the item in the specified slot."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`slot` is the index of the slot to get the item description for. Optional, defaults to `slot()`."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`statusColor([color:number]):number` gets, and optionally sets, the color of the status light."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`color` is the color to set, as a packed `0xRRGGBB` value. Optional."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "Returns the color in effect after the call."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`statusValue([value:number]):number` gets, and optionally sets, how far the status light is filled."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`value` is the fill value to set, in the range of [0, 1]. Optional."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "Returns the fill value in effect after the call."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`detect(side):string` reports what occupies the space on the specified side of the robot."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`side` is the side to look at. See the \"Sides\" section of the [robot device]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "Returns `solid`, `fluid` or `air`. Since only `solid` stops the robot, `detect(side) ~= \"solid\"` tells you a move that way will not be blocked."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`move(direction[,timeout:number]):boolean` tries to move into the specified direction. Blocks until the movement operation has completed."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`direction` is the direction to move in."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`timeout` is how long to wait for, in milliseconds. Optional, defaults to 30000."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "Returns whether the operation was successful. Returns `false` if the timeout ran out first."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`moveAsync(direction[,timeout:number]):boolean` tries to asynchronously move into the specified direction. Blocks until the action was successfully enqueued."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`timeout` is how long to wait for a free slot in the action queue, in milliseconds. Optional, defaults to 30000."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "Returns whether the action was enqueued. Returns `false` if the timeout ran out first."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`turn(direction[,timeout:number]):boolean` tries to turn towards the specified direction. Blocks until the rotation operation has completed."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`direction` is the direction to turn towards."
msgstr ""

#: item/robot.md
msgid "`turnAsync(direction[,timeout:number]):boolean` tries to asynchronously turn into the specified direction. Blocks until the action was successfully enqueued."
msgstr ""

#: item/scanner_module.md
msgid "Scanner Module"
msgstr ""

#: item/scanner_module.md
msgid "![That's inside the room!]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/scanner_module.md
msgid "The scanner module lets [robots]({1}) inspect what is around them. Where the robot's built-in `detect` only rudimentary data, the scanner can provide specifics about the block, the fluid and any creatures and objects."
msgstr ""

#: item/scanner_module.md
msgid "It can also scan the wider area around the robot, measuring block hardness and detecting entities in a 7x7x7 cube around the robot. This has been successfully employed find cavities and ore deposits."
msgstr ""

#: item/scanner_module.md
msgid "Scan results are relative to the robot and aligned to the built-in compass. Also see the \"Position\" section of the [robot device]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: item/scanner_module.md
msgid "Programs control the module through the `scanner` device. See the [scanner device]({1}) reference for its methods and the layout of scan results."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "Serial Interface Card"
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "![Serial experiments]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "The serial interface card allows [computers]({1}) to read and write serial data from a serial port. Serial data is transmitted on a bus established by [network cables]({2})."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "A segment is every endpoint connected through [network connectors]({1}) and [network hubs]({2}). It is *multi-drop*: everything one endpoint writes will be readable by every other endpoint. With more than two participants, software needs to filter."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "In addition to connectivity, this also allows setting an **address.** This is an arbitrary number in [0, 255) that allows identifying different cards on a bus. Hardware ignores it; software may use it to establish a protocol on top of it. This is purely for convenience."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "Baud rate must be configured in software and must match between endpoints on a shared bus. The default after a reset is 9600. Misconfiguration will result in corrupted data. Higher baud rates are more likely to cause collisions unless a protocol prevents this, and weaker systems may not process received data fast enough."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "The ports are named as on Linux, starting at `/dev/ttyS1`. `stty -F /dev/ttyS1 9600` sets the rate. The `oc2.serial` library works the same as on Linux, see below."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "The card is a plain serial port. The first one in a machine is `/dev/ttyS1`, because `ttyS0` is the built-in terminal. Set the rate endpoints on the segment should commonly use:"
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "The `oc2.serial` library wraps that, and also reads the address of the card via the HLAPI:"
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "There is also a MicroPython version with the same API."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "CP/M uses the first card as its reader and punch, so BDOS functions 3 and 4 go through it."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "`STAT CON:=TTY:` switches to the first card as the console, for example to use the computer from a [terminal]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "`STAT CON:=CRT:` goes back to the built-in one."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "`SERBAUD` in `SERIAL.INC` sets another rate from a divisor: 384 is 300 baud, 96 is 1200, 12 is 9600, 6 is 19200, 1 is 115200."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "`TERM` is a dumb terminal on the card: everything typed is written, everything read is printed. `Ctrl-]` quits. Corrupted data prints as `~`."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "`SERIAL.INC` provides utilities for user programs: finding a card's port as well as IO on it, with status results."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "`SERCHAT.Z80` on the system disk is an example program."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "Suggested multi-endpoint framing"
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "The serial port reads and writes raw bytes from and to the bus. To associate data with endpoints, the library expects the following framing:"
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "The checksum is `to + from + length` plus every payload byte, overflowing. Address 255 means everyone."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "Failure modes"
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "Unlike the [network card]({1}), errors are not automatically compensated, so programs must handle them accordingly. The following errors categories exist:"
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "**Overrun**: the machine is not reading the port fast enough, bytes are dropped."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "**Contention**: received data was corrupted because too many senders flooded the bus at the same time."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "**Collision**: sent data was corrupted because too many senders flooded the bus at the same time."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "**Noise**: traffic arrived at a baud rate this port is not set to."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "Error counts can be read via the HLAPI and MLAPI, see below."
msgstr ""

#: item/serial_interface_card.md
msgid "Programs can read the card's address, baud rate, port location and error counters through the `serial` device. See the [serial device]({1}) reference for its methods. **Important**: device order in the HLAPI and MLAPI may differ from the order devices were mounted, i.e. the order they are listed in the device tree/enumeration window."
msgstr ""

#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "Sound Card"
msgstr ""

#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "![Less sound of silence]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "The sound card provides a means for computers to emit arbitrary audio signals. The card supports mono audio at up to 22050 samples per second. CP/M does not support it."
msgstr ""

#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "The first sound card provides three devices, all playing mono audio at 11025 samples per second:"
msgstr ""

#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "`/dev/dsp` takes one unsigned byte per sample."
msgstr ""

#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "`/dev/dspW` takes signed 16-bit samples, little endian."
msgstr ""

#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "`/dev/audio` takes one mu-law encoded byte per sample."
msgstr ""

#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "The format and rate cannot be changed. Audio must be 11025 samples per second. Raw samples can be written to the devices directly, e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "Sound cards typically appear as `/dev/dsp` devices. The simplest way to play audio is to write raw samples to the device:"
msgstr ""

#: item/sound_card.md
msgid "Opened like this, `/dev/dsp` expects mono audio at 8000 samples per second, one unsigned byte per sample. Audio in any other format has to be converted first, or the program playing it has to configure the device to match."
msgstr ""

#: item/tank_operations_module.md
msgid "Tank Operations Module"
msgstr ""

#: item/tank_operations_module.md
msgid "![Bigger on the inside]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/tank_operations_module.md
msgid "The tank operations module provides to [robots]({1}) the ability to insert and extract fluids from tanks in the world. This supports block and entity tanks, as well as container items in the robot's inventory. Without a tank present, given sufficient space, it allows filling or draining fluids into and from the world."
msgstr ""

#: item/tank_operations_module.md
msgid "Programs control the module through the `tank_operations` device. See the [tank operations device]({1}) reference for its methods."
msgstr ""

#: item/wrench.md
msgid "Scrench"
msgstr ""

#: item/wrench.md
msgid "![Screw it!]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: item/wrench.md
msgid "The wrench-screwdriver multi-tool, nicknamed \"Scrench\", can be used to directly access the configuration of some blocks, such as [computers]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: item/wrench.md
msgid "It is required to configure some devices such as [bus interfaces]({1}). It may also be used to quickly dismantle some devices, such as [cables]({2})."
msgstr ""

#: item/wrench.md
msgid "This tool can also be used to remove facades from cables."
msgstr ""

#: item/wrench.md
msgid "It also erases programmed [flash memory]({1}) and [hard drives]({2}), returning them to blank ones."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "The mid-level API (MLAPI) is a low-level friendly way of interacting with devices. Its primary consumer is programs running on [CP/M]({1}), but programs on [Linux]({2}) can make use of it, as well, see below. OpenOS II does not support it. It usually makes available a subset of the devices accessible via the [high-level API]({3}) (HLAPI), in a form an 8-bit machine can access comfortably: a function is picked by number, and its arguments and results are plain bytes."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "For the function codes each device offers, see the [list of devices]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Everything here is done through ports, so it works from assembly. Two libraries on the boot disk save you writing the tedious parts, `DEVLIB.INC` for finding general devices and `OCAPI.INC` for finding and calling mid-level API devices. Include them at the end of your sources:"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Finding a Device"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Devices are findable through an enumeration window at a fixed port. `DEVS.COM` prints what's on there, which is a good first check that a device is connected at all."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Every MLAPI device answers on the same port and is told apart by an MLAPI-device index. `OCFIND` in `OCAPI.INC` does the lookup by device name, device type or bus interface label:"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`HL` points at a list of names, each terminated by a zero, with one extra, final zero indicating the end of the list. A device must match all, e.g. a label and a device name: `db 'storage',0,'ITEMS',0,0`"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`B` is which one to find, counting from zero, for when there are multiple matching devices"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "On success the carry flag is clear, `A` holds the port to talk to and `E` holds the MLAPI-device index"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "On failure the carry flag is set"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Write `E` to the `OCSEL` register to pick the device, then work from the port in `A`. Selecting stays valid until you select something else. Read the index again after devices change on the bus, since it can shift."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Every computer and robot has a [`SYSTEM`]({1}) device. Other devices identify items, fluids and blocks by ids to save bus bandwidth. Corresponding names can be looked up using this `SYSTEM` device and vice versa. It is always MLAPI-device index 0, so it can be used without prior `OCFIND`."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Making a Call"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "The port `OCFIND` returned has four registers, named in `OCAPI.INC`:"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`OCSEL` selects the MLAPI-device"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`OCFUN` takes a function code and starts a call"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`OCDAT` takes argument bytes, and gives back result bytes"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`OCSTA` reports status, and takes `OCEXEC` to run the call or `OCABRT` to abandon it"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "A call goes: write the function code to `OCFUN`, write each argument byte to `OCDAT`, write `OCEXEC` to `OCSTA`. Then poll `OCSTA` until `OCBUSY` clears, and read result bytes from `OCDAT` while `OCDAV` is set."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Many calls may take some time to execute, so make sure to poll and don't just continue. Only one call runs at a time, and while `OCBUSY` is set only writes to `OCSTA` are accepted."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "When `OCERR` is set in the status, the call failed and `OCDAT` gives the reason:"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`OCENOD`, no such MLAPI-device"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`OCENOF`, no such function"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`OCEARG`, the arguments were not what the function wanted"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`OCEINT`, the device failed internally"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "The registers hold a single selection and a single call, so keep to one at a time and make no calls from interrupt handlers."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Waiting for Events"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Some devices report changes by themselves, such as the [redstone interface]({1}) when the signal it receives changes. The device documentation found in the [list of devices]({2}) usually lists the device's events."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "The following registers deal with events:"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`OCEVC` switches them on: write `OCEVQ` to have events queued, and add `OCEVI` to also get an interrupt"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`OCEVD` gives back the oldest event, one byte per read: the MLAPI-device index, the event code, then a two-byte value"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Bit `OCEVP` in `OCEVC` is set while events are waiting. Events arriving while the queue is full are lost, and bit `OCEVO` is set every time it happens."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "On the Z80, with the interrupt switched on, `HALT` sleeps until the next event, and your interrupt handler reads it. `REDWAIT.Z80` on the boot disk does this: it waits for a redstone signal to change, then prints the side and the new level. Build it like `REDSTN.Z80` below."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`REDSTN.Z80` on the boot disk drives a [redstone interface]({1}), and its comments include the equivalent C, if that reads more easily. Writing to a floppy needs a [disk drive]({2}). Build it while staying on `A:`:"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "The first command assembles the source. `/O` puts the result in `B:REDSTN.REL` and `/E` drops the error log. The second links that into `B:REDSTN.COM`, which CP/M can run:"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "This sets the output on side 1, upwards, to 15. Put a redstone lamp above the computer and it lights up. Leave off the level to read the levels back instead:"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "To write your own, `ED` on the boot disk creates and edits source files, as in `ED B:PROG.Z80`."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "On Linux"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Linux offers the same registers as a memory page in the file `/dev/uio0`, which needs `root` privileges to open. The C header `mlapi.h` provides utilities to find and map this memory area; layout is then the same as described above. It also provides a helper to find a device by its names, `mlapi_find(0, \"storage\", \"ITEMS\")`, which matches devices the same way as `OCFIND`, and to make a call. It lives in `/mnt/builtin/include`, where `tcc` should pick it up automatically."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`/mnt/builtin/example/redstone.c` does the same as `REDSTN.Z80` does on the Z80. Run it directly:"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "Without arguments, it does what `REDWAIT.Z80` does: `mlapi_events_wait` in `mlapi.h` sleeps until the next event."
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "`/mnt/builtin/example/devices.c` lists every device with its index and names:"
msgstr ""

#: mlapi.md
msgid "There is one selection and one call for the whole computer. Only let one program use the page at a time."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "Networking"
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "To set up a [computer]({1}) network, use [network connectors]({2}) and [network cables]({3}) to connect the connectors. Finally, make sure a [network interface card]({4}) is installed in each computer that should be part of the network."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "Only [Linux]({1}) supports networking."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "Connectors have to be attached to any face but the front face of a computer. This allows the network card installed in it to send and receive packets through the connector."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "Connectors connected with cables will forward packets to each other. For more complex network setups, a [network hub]({1}) may be necessary. It allows connecting multiple connectors with each other."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "Hops"
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "Network packets can only travel a certain maximum number of \"hops\". A hop can generally be understood as a single forwarding operation. For example, a connector forwarding a packet uses one hop. A network hub forwarding a packet to each connector the packet did not arrive from uses one hop. Once the number of remaining hops reaches zero, the packet is no longer forwarded. This avoids packets going in circles forever for networks with cycles. That said, this is a safeguard mechanism. No network should contain cycles, as this will also lead to the same packet arriving multiple times on receiving computers."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "Computer Setup"
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "To set up a computer for networking, first ensure a network card is present. After this, run the command `setup-network.lua`. This will provide a wizard to configure how the computer should connect to the network. Alternatively, if you know what you're doing, set up network as you would on any regular Linux installation."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "DHCP"
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "DHCP is a protocol which allows a simplified network setup. Only one computer will need to have a statically configured network address, all other computers in the network may have their addresses assigned to them automatically. When going for this setup, ensure there is only a single computer that acts as a DHCP server. Also ensure that no computer uses a static IP address that falls into the range of dynamically distributed IP addresses."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "`setup-network.lua` starts the DHCP server when you configure a computer as one. It does not survive a reboot. See the \"Daemons\" section below for how to start it again, or how to make it start on its own."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "Once you have a network setup running, with multiple computers in one network, you have all the options in the world. For example, you can copy files between computers using `scp`, log in to a remote computer using `ssh` and write custom network programs in Lua using the `socket` library. For samples on how to use the `socket` library, please see the [samples in the official repository]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "Note that `scp` and `ssh` are the client tools. Connecting to a computer *from* elsewhere needs a server running on it. See the \"Daemons\" section below."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "The Internet"
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "For access outside the game, a network needs an [internet gateway]({1}) on it. Computers then use their ordinary [network interface cards]({2}) and route through it."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "The gateway offers no DHCP and no name server, so set an address, a route and a name server yourself. See the [internet gateway]({1}) entry for an example."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "Daemons"
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "The network daemons are shipped but not started at boot. Start the one you need by hand:"
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "`/etc/init.d/dropbear start` for the `ssh` and `scp` server"
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "`/etc/init.d/telnet start` for the `telnet` server"
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "`/etc/init.d/dnsmasq start` for the DHCP server"
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "The `ssh` server refuses to let anyone in while an account has a blank password, which is what `root` ships with. Set one with `passwd` before connecting for the first time. The `telnet` server has no such restriction, and accepts the blank password as-is, so anyone who can connect to the computer can log into it."
msgstr ""

#: networking.md
msgid "None of these survive a reboot. To start one at boot, give its script a boot-sequence prefix:"
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "Robotics"
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "[Robots]({1}) are [computers]({2}) that can move. Everything in the [basics]({3}) and [HLAPI]({4})/[MLAPI]({5}) entries applies to them as well. For the full method reference, see the [robot]({6}) entry."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "Building a Robot"
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "A robot is configured much like a computer, with two differences."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "It cannot connect to [bus cables]({1}), so all its devices must be installed in the robot itself. In place of cards, robots take modules such as the [block operations module]({2})."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "It has two inventories. One for internal components: the processor, memory, modules. This one must be maintained by hand. Another, regular inventory, which is the robot's main storage. This is what automated interaction works with, such as when using a hopper. It also has a built-in tank, which may be automated like the storage inventory, e.g. using a transposer."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "A robot draws energy from its internal storage for as long as its computer runs. Consumption depends on the installed components; moving and turning cost nothing on top of that. When it runs out, the computer stops with an out-of-energy error, wherever the robot is."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "Recharge using a [charger]({1}). A robot can move onto one by itself, so an unattended recharge is easy to script. Breaking a robot and picking it up preserves its energy, its inventory, including the installed components, and its tank."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "Action Queue"
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "`move` and `turn` do not move the robot synchronously. They append an action to a queue and return whether there was room for it. The robot then runs the queue one action at a time. Moving one block takes a second, turning ninety degrees takes a second."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "Every enqueued action gets an id, available from `getLastActionId()`. Once the robot has finished it, `getActionResult(id)` reports `SUCCESS` or `FAILURE`, and the computer receives a `robotActionCompleted` event with the same information. Until then the result is `INCOMPLETE`. Only the last sixteen results are kept."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "A move into an obstacle is not rejected up front. The robot starts moving, hits the obstacle, returns to its original position and reports `FAILURE`. Shutting the computer down clears both the queue and the stored results."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "Directions and Sides"
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "Movement and module operations use different terminology."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "Movement takes `forward`, `backward`, `upward` and `downward`; rotation takes `left` and `right`. Module operations take a side, which is one of `front`, `up` and `down`. There is no `forward` side and no `front` direction."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "Both accept short forms. `up`, `down`, `back` and `ahead` work for movement, and the single letters `f`, `b`, `u`, `d`, `l` and `r` work wherever the corresponding long form does."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "Robots have a built-in compass and keep track of where they are. `getFacing()` reports `north`, `east`, `south` or `west`. `getPosition()` reports the offset from the robot's origin in blocks: `x` grows towards east, `y` upward and `z` towards south."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "The origin is initialized when the robot is placed. `calibratePosition()` resets it to where the robot is at that moment. The position stays correct even when the robot is moved by external measures."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "The robot Library"
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "Driving the action queue by hand is tedious, so OpenOS II and Linux ship a `robot` library that waits for completion:"
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "`move` and `turn` block until the action has completed and return whether it succeeded. `moveAsync` and `turnAsync` block only until the action is in the queue. All four take an optional timeout in milliseconds, defaulting to thirty seconds, and return `false` if it runs out. For the asynchronous pair, a timeout means the queue stayed full for that long."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "On Linux, an equivalent library is available for MicroPython. On CP/M, use the `robot` device through the [MLAPI]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "This walks the robot around a three by three square:"
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "This digs a straight tunnel, stopping as soon as it cannot get any further. It needs a block operations module and a pickaxe in the selected slot:"
msgstr ""

#: robotics.md
msgid "A move takes a second, usually enough for the module's cooldown to expire. On a block that takes longer to break, the next `excavate` returns `false` without doing anything, the move that follows fails, and the loop stops early. Retry the excavation to dig through those as well."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "CP/M 2.2 runs on the [Z80 processor]({1}). It is a much cheaper, but also much more limited environment. Programs are written in Z80 assembly, and assembled and linked on the computer itself. It supports [disk drives]({2}), [serial interface cards]({3}) and [MLAPI]({4}) devices. Hard drives, network cards and the other native devices are ignored without effect."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
#: systems/linux.md
#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Parts"
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
#: systems/linux.md
#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Build the computer as described in [getting started]({1}), using these parts:"
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
#: systems/linux.md
#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "1x [computer]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "1x [Z80 processor]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "1x **CP/M 2.2** [flash memory]({1}), crafted from flash memory and a Z80 processor"
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "1x [disk drive]({1}) on the bus, holding a **CP/M 2.2** [floppy]({2}), crafted from a floppy and a Z80 processor"
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "A [robot]({1}) has a floppy slot of its own, so it can have a system disk without a disk drive."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Starting Up"
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "Power the computer and turn it on. The loader in the flash memory finds the system floppy and boots CP/M to its prompt, `A>`. Run `DIR` to see what came on the disk:"
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "`DEVS.COM` prints the devices the computer can see."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "`DEVLIB.INC`, `OCAPI.INC` and `SERIAL.INC` are libraries for your own programs."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "`REDSTN.Z80`, `REDWAIT.Z80` and `SERCHAT.Z80` are example programs."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "`ZMAC.COM` and `ZML.COM` are the assembler and the linker."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "`ED.COM` is a line editor, `STAT.COM` shows free space and device assignments."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "`TERM.COM` is a terminal for the serial interface card."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
#: systems/linux.md
#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Drives"
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "Drives are lettered in the order they are found, starting at `A:`. The floppy the computer booted from always comes first. Add another [disk drive]({1}) with a blank [floppy]({2}) for more room, it shows up as `B:`. Type `B:` to switch to it."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "The prompt has a few built-in commands: `DIR` lists files, `TYPE` prints a text file, `ERA` deletes files and `REN NEW.TXT=OLD.TXT` renames a file."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "Building Programs"
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "`ED B:PROG.Z80` creates and edits a source file. To assemble and link a program onto `B:`, while staying on `A:`:"
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "The first command assembles the source, the second links it into `B:REDSTN.COM`. The [mid-level API]({1}) entry explains the options and what this example does."
msgstr ""

#: systems/cpm.md
msgid "To control devices such as the [redstone interface]({1}), see the [mid-level API]({2}). To use the computer from a [terminal]({3}), see the [serial interface card]({4})."
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "This guide describes how to get a [computer]({1}) up and running. Building and powering a computer works the same for every operating system (OS), it is described below. What parts to install and how to use the system depends on the OS. Each has its own guide:"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[OpenOS II]({1}) runs on the [RISC-V processor]({2}). It is a small Lua-based system, it boots with a single 2M memory module."
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[Linux]({1}) runs on the RISC-V processor as well. It offers the most features and needs the most memory and storage."
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[CP/M]({1}) runs on the [Z80 processor]({2}). It makes for a cheap start, with programs written in assembly."
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "It is strongly suggested to start with OpenOS II, as it is the most accessible. To go deeper, pick Linux for networking, MicroPython or C, or CP/M for assembly on an 8-bit machine. For the concepts behind processors and devices, see the [basics]({1})."
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "Building"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "First things first, you need an actual computer case, plus the components listed in the guide for your OS. Once you have all this, place down the computer. Open its inventory screen using a [wrench]({1}). Alternatively, open the terminal screen, then use the toggle button to the left to switch to the inventory screen. Here, place the processor, flash memory and, for OpenOS II and Linux, the memory and hard drive into the computer. CP/M boots from a floppy in a disk drive on the bus. A computer without a processor will not start."
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "![Computer inventory]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "Starting"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "To power up your freshly built computer, you'll usually need to supply it with some power. Have a look at the energy bar to the left of the terminal or inventory screen. Its tooltip informs you of the current amount of energy stored in the computer, and the amount of energy it requires per tick to keep running."
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "![Computer energy info]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "When you've ensured the required amount of energy is available, switch to the terminal screen and hit the power button to the top left. Alternatively use the computer while sneaking. The computer should now boot! Continue in the guide for your OS."
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "Feature Overview"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "Different operating systems support different hardware. This is a brief overview of what works where:"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[Hard drives]({1}): OpenOS II, Linux"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[Floppies]({1}) in a [disk drive]({2}): all"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[Flash drive]({1}): OpenOS II, Linux"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[File import/export]({1}): OpenOS II, Linux"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[Network]({1}): Linux"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[Sound card]({1}): OpenOS II, Linux"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[Projector]({1}): OpenOS II (programs only), Linux (with a console)"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[Keyboard]({1}): OpenOS II (programs only), Linux (console input)"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[Serial interface card]({1}): all"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[Terminal]({1}) as a console: OpenOS II, Linux, CP/M"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[HLAPI]({1}) devices: OpenOS II, Linux"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[MLAPI]({1}) devices: CP/M, Linux"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "[Robots]({1}): all"
msgstr ""

#: systems/index.md
msgid "Good luck, and most importantly, have fun!"
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "Linux runs on the [RISC-V processor]({1}). It is a small distribution with the common command line tools from busybox, Lua, MicroPython and the `tcc` C compiler. It supports every device in this manual."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "1x [RISC-V processor]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "1x **Sedna Firmware** [flash memory]({1}), crafted from flash memory and a RISC-V processor"
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "1x **Sedna Linux** [hard drive]({1}), crafted from an 8M hard drive and a RISC-V processor"
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "3x 4M [memory]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "Linux needs at least 8M of memory, 10M are highly recommended."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "Logging In"
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "Power the computer, turn it on and wait until you're prompted for a login."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "![Login prompt]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "Enter `root` as the user name and press enter. The `root` account has no password."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "The System"
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "The shell and most tools come from busybox, so the usual commands such as `ls`, `cp`, `cat` and `less` work as on any other Linux. To edit files, use `vi` or `nano`."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "To run a Lua script, use `lua script.lua`. Run `lua` without arguments for the interactive prompt. MicroPython works the same, via `micropython`. Compile and run C programs with `tcc -run program.c`."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "To turn the computer off from the shell, run `poweroff`. `reboot` restarts it."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "The boot drive is `/dev/vda`. Additional [hard drives]({1}), [disk drives]({2}) and [flash drives]({3}) follow as `/dev/vdb`, `/dev/vdc` and so on."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "They are *not* automatically mounted. Use `mount /dev/vdb /mnt/data` to mount tem. The directory must exist and should be empty. Use `umount /mnt/data` unmounts it."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "A new drive needs a file system first. Run `mke2fs /dev/vdb` to create one. Formatting a drive *erases all data on it!*"
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "Floppies can be swapped at any time. Unmount before removing a floppy, to avoid data loss."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "To control devices such as the [redstone interface]({1}) from Lua or MicroPython, use the `devices` library, see the [high-level API]({2}). C programs can also use the [mid-level API]({3}). `lsdev` lists the connected devices."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "The built-in file system at `/mnt/builtin` also provides `import` and `export` for the [file import/export card]({1}), and the `robot` library for [robots]({2})."
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Clocks"
msgstr ""

#: systems/linux.md
msgid "Computers provide two hardware clock (RTC) devices. The first one counts time in a scale most users will think in. It is used by default, for example by `date` and `time`. The second one measures time as it works in the world the computer runs in. To obtain the current world time, use `hwclock -f /dev/rtc1`."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "OpenOS II runs on the [RISC-V processor]({1}). It is a small operating system with a shell and programs written in Lua. It should feel fairly familiar if you've used OpenOS before. It needs far less memory and storage than [Linux]({2}), but has no networking."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "1x **OpenOS II** [hard drive]({1}), crafted from a 2M hard drive and a RISC-V processor"
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "1x 2M [memory]({1})"
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Power the computer and turn it on. OpenOS II boots and shows its prompt, `/ # `. There is no login, there are no users. The prompt shows the current directory. Well done, you now have a computer that's ready for use!"
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "The shell works much like one would on Linux:"
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "`ls /bin /mnt/builtin/bin` lists the installed programs. Most programs print their usage with `--help`."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Up and Down go through the history, Tab completes commands and paths."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "`|` passes the output of one program to the next, `>` and `>>` write it to a file, `<` reads from one."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "`;`, `&&` and `||` chain commands. `*` and `?` match file names."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "`cmd &` runs a command in the background. `jobs` lists these, `kill` ends one."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Ctrl+C stops the running program."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "To turn the computer off, run `shutdown`. `reboot` restarts it."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Commands placed in `/.shrc` run whenever the shell starts, e.g. aliases (`alias ll='ls -l'`) or a keyboard layout."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Writing Programs"
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "To edit a file, run `edit <file>`. Ctrl+S saves, Ctrl+W closes."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "A program is a Lua file. Use `lua <file>` to run them, `lua` alone starts the interactive prompt. Programs in `/bin`, `/mnt/builtin/bin` and the current directory run by name, without the `.lua` extension. The program gets its arguments as `...`:"
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "`require` loads libraries from `/lib`, from the built-in file system at `/mnt/builtin/lib/lua`, and from the current directory."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Drives are mounted automatically, at `/mnt/<label>` if the medium has a label, else at `/mnt/1`, `/mnt/2` and so on."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "A new drive needs a file system first. Run `format /dev/vdb` to create one, optionally passing a label as a second argument. It's also possible to use `label` to change the label later. Formatting a drive *erases all data on it!*"
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Floppies can be swapped at any time. Close open files on a floppy before removing it, to avoid data loss."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "To control devices such as the [redstone interface]({1}), use the `devices` library, see the [high-level API]({2}). `lsdev` lists the connected devices."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "The [sound card]({1}), [projector]({2}) and [keyboard]({3}) are available to programs. See their respective entries on how to use them."
msgstr ""

#: systems/openos2.md
msgid "Computers provide two hardware clock (RTC) devices. The first one counts time in a scale most users will think in. It is used by default, for example by `date` and `time`. The second one measures time as it works in the world the computer runs in. To obtain the current world time, use `date -w`."
msgstr ""
